    summary: "Urothelial carcinoma of the bladder was the ninth most common cancer worldwide in GLOBOCAN 2024 (eighth if non-melanoma skin cancer is left out), and a 2003 review of US costs found it the most expensive to manage per patient, because three quarters present as non-muscle-invasive disease that recurs for years and demands lifelong cystoscopy. Smoking causes about half of cases. Muscle-invasive disease (25%) has required radical cystectomy with neoadjuvant cisplatin since the 2000s, and metastatic disease relied on platinum chemotherapy for four decades.\n\nBetween 2019 and 2026 the field was rebuilt. In metastatic disease, enfortumab vedotin plus pembrolizumab nearly doubled survival over chemotherapy (EV-302, 2023). The same pair then moved around surgery: EV-303 in cisplatin-ineligible patients (EFS HR 0.40, approved November 2025) and EV-304 in cisplatin-eligible patients (approved July 2026), after NIAGARA had already established perioperative durvalumab (approved March 2025); in November 2026 the FDA also approved durvalumab with enfortumab vedotin around surgery for cisplatin-ineligible patients (VOLGA). Adjuvant nivolumab (CheckMate 274) and ctDNA-guided adjuvant atezolizumab (IMvigor011, approved May 2026 and described by its maker as the first approval of a ctDNA-guided therapy) cover the post-cystectomy space. In non-muscle-invasive disease, the BCG-unresponsive population gained four bladder-sparing options (pembrolizumab, nadofaragene firadenovec, N-803 + BCG, and the gemcitabine-eluting device TAR-200), with a rolling US application for the oncolytic virus cretostimogene due to complete by March 2027, and durvalumab + BCG became the first systemic immunotherapy in BCG-naive disease (POTOMAC, May 2026). Erdafitinib remains the only targeted drug, for FGFR3-altered tumours after immunotherapy.\n\nWhat is next: bladder preservation for complete responders to perioperative EV + pembrolizumab; sequencing after EV + pembrolizumab (platinum, HER2 ADCs such as disitamab vedotin, TROP2 and bispecific ADCs, sac-TMT); urine tumour DNA to reduce cystoscopy; resolving BCG shortages with recombinant strains and alternatives; and understanding why some checkpoint inhibitors given alongside BCG (durvalumab, sasanlimab) add to it while another (atezolizumab) did not.",
